  "body": "https://html.spec.whatwg.org/dev/introduction.html#typographic-conventions\r\n\r\nCurrently, the following typographic conventions are the same style (italic):\r\n\r\n> *This is an example.*\r\n> The defining instance of a term is marked up like *this*.\r\n> Variables are marked up like *this*.\r\n\r\nFollowing styles are the same (normal), too:\r\n> This is a definition, requirement, or explanation.\r\n> This is a note to authors describing the usage of an interface.\r\n\r\nThese are also the same (monospace):\r\n> The defining instance of an element, attribute, or API is marked up like `this`. \r\n> Other code fragments are marked up like `this`.\r\n\r\nBecause these styles can't be distinguished, I feel that they are preferably different each other like <a href=\"https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/introduction.html#typographic-conventions\">the spec</a>.",
